// Liu Jin's extended Q&A bank for slides 12–15, rendered after the segment 4 Q&A: { tip, groups: [{ title, rows }] }
const QA_EXTENDED = {
  4: { title: 'Liu Jin: extended Q&A bank (slides 12–15)',
    tip: 'Rows above stay the first answers. These are the likely follow-ups, checked against the repository on 20 Oct. Answers in brackets are notes for Liu Jin, not to be read out.',
    groups: [
      { title: 'Slide 12: physical decisions', rows: [
        ['Why ARM? Did that cause problems?', 'ARM Fargate is about 20 percent cheaper for the same size. Java 21 runs natively on ARM, and CI builds every image for arm64, so there was nothing to port.', 'Liu Jin'],
        ['You use Fargate Spot. What happens when AWS takes a task back?', 'The minimum tasks always run on regular Fargate; only extra tasks use Spot, weighted three to one. If a Spot task is reclaimed, ECS starts a replacement and the load balancer drains the old one. A failed deployment rolls back by itself through the circuit breaker.', 'Liu Jin'],
        ['How did you measure 49 cents?', 'From AWS Cost Explorer, filtered to the runtime services (database, cache and containers) for 15 to 17 October, seven sessions. It excludes the permanent edge and the development server. While up, the environment costs about 12 to 20 cents an hour. (No evidence file in the repository: keep a Cost Explorer screenshot ready.)', 'Liu Jin'],
        ['Isn’t one NAT instance a single point of failure?', 'Yes, for outbound traffic. It’s a small fck-nat instance in an auto-scaling group of one: if it dies, a replacement takes over the same network interface, so no routes change. During that gap, calls to AWS APIs pause; events stay in the outbox and are retried, so nothing is lost. In production we’d use NAT gateways or VPC endpoints. (Image layers use the S3 gateway endpoint; ECR API, SSM, SNS, SQS and CloudWatch go through the NAT.)', 'Liu Jin'],
        ['You destroy the environment every time. What about the data?', 'It’s a disposable test environment: the database is rebuilt each session, with no backups, and the demo data is reseeded automatically. Production would turn on backups, point-in-time recovery, Multi-AZ and deletion protection.', 'Liu Jin'],
        ['How long does a rebuild take?', 'About 26 to 32 minutes, of which about 15 are spent waiting for the SNS topic (AISS-06). On the day we start early.', 'Liu Jin'],
      ] },
      { title: 'Slide 13: physical view', rows: [
        ['Is traffic encrypted end to end?', 'Users reach CloudFront over HTTPS, and services talk to the database and the cache over TLS; the database enforces it, and the cache also needs a password. The hop from CloudFront to the load balancer is plain HTTP today, locked down by CloudFront’s IP ranges and a secret header; moving it to HTTPS with a certificate is a known next step. (Do not claim that hop is encrypted.)', 'Liu Jin'],
        ['What if someone finds the load balancer’s address?', 'Its security group only accepts CloudFront’s IP ranges, and even from CloudFront a request without the secret header gets a 403.', 'Liu Jin'],
        ['Do you use a WAF?', 'Not yet. The next step is AWS WAF on CloudFront, with managed rules and rate limits.', 'Liu Jin'],
        ['Where are the secrets?', 'In Parameter Store, encrypted. Database passwords are generated as write-only values, so they never land in Terraform state, and nothing is in the code. (The cache’s auth token is in Terraform state: do not say “no secrets in state”.)', 'Liu Jin'],
      ] },
      { title: 'Slide 14: scaling', rows: [
        ['Why scale on requests and not CPU?', 'Browsing is driven by requests, and we measured capacity per task in requests. CPU reacts later and is noisier. Commerce scales out after 60 seconds and in after 180 seconds, so it doesn’t flap.', 'Liu Jin'],
        ['How does notification scale?', 'On queue backlog: about 50 waiting messages per task, between one and four tasks. Identity stays at one task.', 'Liu Jin'],
        ['How many database connections do six commerce tasks use?', 'Each task has a pool of ten connections. At full scale, six commerce, four notification and one identity task, that’s up to 110, close to what a micro database allows. On 19 October the peak was 50 connections with the database under 19 percent CPU. Past that we’d shrink the pools, move to a bigger instance or add RDS Proxy. (Before the talk, read the real limit with infra/swe5001/scripts/connection-budget.sh on the live environment; the budget was not checked formally before V3.)', 'Liu Jin'],
      ] },
      { title: 'Slide 15: data', rows: [
        ['Why one database server and not one per service?', 'Cost. Each service still has its own database and its own roles, and moving one to its own server needs no code change (AISS-05).', 'Liu Jin'],
        ['How do you stop commerce reading identity’s data?', 'Identity’s tables are in a separate database that commerce isn’t allowed to connect to. Each database has an owner role and an app role, and the permissions are re-applied every time a service starts.', 'Liu Jin'],
        ['What exactly is the lock order?', 'Team first, then the purchase, then the payment, each with SELECT … FOR UPDATE. Because every code path locks in the same order, two buyers can’t deadlock: the first to lock the team gets the last seat, and the second sees the team is full.', 'Liu Jin'],
        ['How does the scheduled-job lock work?', 'Each job holds a short lease in a database table: two minutes, renewed every third of that. If another task holds the lease, it skips the run; if renewal fails, the batch stops. Only commerce runs scheduled jobs.', 'Liu Jin'],
        ['Why the database for that lock, not Redis?', 'The database is already the source of truth for these jobs, so the lock lives next to the data it protects and needs no extra dependency.', 'Liu Jin'],
        ['Do you store card data?', 'No. Payment uses Stripe’s hosted checkout, so card numbers never reach our servers; we keep only Stripe’s payment references.', 'Liu Jin'],
      ] },
    ] },
};

